1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,688
02:00. Ferry-booking logs in the
aggregator. Four pods. A customer says
checkout charged twice.

2
00:00:08,807 --> 00:00:20,664
You search the booking reference and get
fragments: one line from pod A at payment
start, nothing from the gateway callback,
an ERROR on pod C without a booking id, a
stack trace on pod B for an unrelated
sailing.

3
00:00:20,783 --> 00:00:32,783
Missing correlation IDs turn a distributed
request into archaeology. Boot's default
console pattern is fine for a laptop; it
is not an incident strategy for multi-pod
ferry checkout.

4
00:00:32,903 --> 00:00:45,143
Boot's default logging uses Logback behind
SLF4J, with logging.level.* and
logging.pattern.* properties, and optional
logback-spring.xml for profiles and
advanced appenders.

5
00:00:45,263 --> 00:00:58,847
The framework lesson is not "how to
print." It is structured, correlatable
output that survives multi-pod reality —
and the MDC (Mapped Diagnostic Context) as
the carrier for per-request identifiers on
a thread.

6
00:00:58,967 --> 00:01:12,311
Look at this example. <!-- logback-
spring.xml --> Look at this java for a
moment. Walk the filter and encoder.
OncePerRequestFilter guarantees one
execution per dispatch.

7
00:01:12,431 --> 00:01:28,055
Read X-Correlation-Id if a gateway already
set it; otherwise mint a UUID.
MDC.put("correlationId", cid) stores the
value on the current thread. res.setHeader
returns the id to clients and support
tools.

8
00:01:28,175 --> 00:01:43,127
try/finally with MDC.remove prevents leaks
onto pooled threads — a classic bug where
later requests inherit someone else's
correlation id. The Logback pattern
%X{correlationId} pulls from MDC into JSON
as corr.

9
00:01:43,247 --> 00:02:00,287
springProperty reads
spring.application.name so every line
names the ferry service. Runtime for one
checkout. Request enters pod A; filter
assigns id c-9f3. Payment service logs
"charging" with corr=c-9f3.

10
00:02:00,407 --> 00:02:10,679
WebClient/Feign to the payment gateway
should forward X-Correlation-Id — if it
does not, the callback lands on pod C with
a *new* id and your aggregator cannot join
charge and callback.

11
00:02:10,799 --> 00:02:23,471
With propagation, refund decision logs
share c-9f3 across pods. Without MDC,
Boot's nice default console pattern still
leaves you string-matching timestamps and
booking refs that half the lines omit.

12
00:02:23,591 --> 00:02:35,783
logging.level.com.ferry=DEBUG raises
detail for one package without
recompiling; Actuator's loggers endpoint
can change levels live when secured —
useful at 02:00 if you already have
correlation.

13
00:02:35,903 --> 00:02:48,431
Failure mode symptoms: duplicate charge
investigation finds four unrelated corr
values for one customer journey — header
not propagated on outbound calls, or MDC
cleared too early, or async work (@Async,

14
00:02:48,551 --> 00:02:53,663
reactive) lost MDC because it changed
threads without copying context.

15
00:02:53,783 --> 00:03:00,816
Symptom of MDC leak: support searches one
id and sees fragments of other customers'
checkouts on the same pod.

16
00:03:00,935 --> 00:03:12,815
Symptom of logging.level.root=DEBUG in
prod during panic: disk full, log pipeline
lag, real ERROR lines drowned — and
sometimes added latency from logging
volume. Trade-offs.

17
00:03:12,935 --> 00:03:24,047
JSON + correlation costs a dependency and
filter discipline; it buys queryable
incidents. Text patterns are easier to
read in a terminal and worse in
aggregators.

18
00:03:24,167 --> 00:03:38,399
Putting booking ids only in free-text
messages fails when the message format
changes; first-class MDC fields stay
stable. Sampling debug logs or using
traces (later episodes) handles volume
better than permanent DEBUG.

19
00:03:38,519 --> 00:03:53,303
One ferry-specific discipline: log the
booking reference as an MDC field too
(MDC.put("bookingRef", ...) once the ref
exists), not only inside message strings.
Aggregators index fields; they do not
reliably parse prose.

20
00:03:53,423 --> 00:04:03,263
Correlation id joins hops; booking ref
joins business. Together they turn
"charged twice" from four pods into two
queries instead of a scavenger hunt.

21
00:04:03,383 --> 00:04:17,351
Misconception unique to logging in Boot:
"Setting logging.level.root=DEBUG in
production is the responsible way to 'get
more detail' during an incident." It
floods disks, hides the signal, and can
itself cause latency.

22
00:04:17,471 --> 00:04:27,719
Prefer targeted packages, already-
correlated IDs, and metrics/traces for
volume. Checkout archaeology becomes a
single query on corr.

23
00:04:27,839 --> 00:04:38,639
Finance still deploys the same ferry
artifact to staging and prod with
different payment merchant IDs — and last
week staging charged the prod merchant
because the Boot profile document never
activated.

24
00:04:38,759 --> 00:04:43,295
Profile-specific YAML in Boot packaging is
the next seam to tighten.
